PROJECT NAME: MachineSentry AI

TAGLINE:
"Detect Anomalies Early. Maintain Machines Smarter."

PROJECT CATEGORY:
AI/ML + Industrial IoT + Predictive Maintenance

PROJECT PURPOSE:
Build a functional, professional, responsive web application
that demonstrates affordable machine-condition monitoring using
audio analysis, machine-learning-based anomaly detection, and
optional vibration-sensor data.

The target users are small factories, workshops, maintenance
technicians, engineering students, and small industrial businesses
that cannot afford expensive monitoring systems for every machine.

This project should be suitable for a college engineering
hackathon and should demonstrate real technical depth.

IMPORTANT:
Do not create only a landing page or a static dashboard.

Build a working end-to-end prototype with actual data processing,
anomaly detection, interactive visualizations, persistent demo
records, and meaningful error handling.

Do not pretend that simulated data is live sensor data.
Clearly distinguish real uploaded recordings, simulated readings,
and actual hardware measurements.

Prioritize a working core application over decorative animations.

====================================================
1. THE CORE PROBLEM
====================================================

Small factories often operate motors, pumps, fans, and other
machines without sophisticated condition-monitoring equipment.

Unexpected machine problems can lead to:

- Production interruptions.
- Unexpected maintenance costs.
- Equipment damage.
- Difficulties identifying abnormal operating behaviour early.
- Dependence on expensive industrial monitoring systems.

Traditional supervised machine-learning systems often require
labelled examples of different machine failures.

However, small factories may have many examples of normal
operation and very few examples of actual faults.

The proposed solution is MachineSentry AI.

MachineSentry learns the characteristics of normal machine
operation and detects unusual sound patterns.

If vibration data is available, the application can analyse
vibration features as an additional signal.

The application generates an anomaly score and provides
evidence-based explanations to help a maintenance technician
decide whether an inspection is appropriate.

CORE TECHNICAL IDEA:

Learn a baseline from normal operating data.

Compare new observations against that baseline.

Detect unusual patterns.

Display the anomaly score, relevant features, and a suggested
inspection action.

IMPORTANT SCIENTIFIC LIMITATION:

An anomaly is not automatically a machine failure.

The system must not claim to predict the exact failure type,
remaining useful life, or time until breakdown unless those
capabilities are separately trained and validated using
appropriate labelled data.

Describe the initial product as an anomaly-detection and
maintenance-support system, not a certified diagnostic tool.

