9. ANOMALY-DETECTION ENGINE
====================================================

Implement a real, baseline-driven anomaly-detection pipeline.

INITIAL MODEL:
Isolation Forest using features extracted from normal-operation
recordings.

OPTIONAL COMPARISON:
A statistical baseline or One-Class SVM.

The user should be able to:

- Select a machine.
- Choose its trained baseline.
- Upload a new recording.
- Extract its features.
- Calculate an anomaly score.
- Compare the result against the baseline.
- Review which features differ from normal observations.

The application must distinguish:

A. Model decision score.
B. Normalized display score.
C. Alert threshold.
D. User-configured alert sensitivity.

Do not present an arbitrary number between 0 and 100 as a
scientifically calibrated probability of failure.

If the model produces a decision score, explain its meaning.

If a normalized display score is used, label it as an
anomaly indicator rather than a probability of failure.

The alert threshold should be configurable and documented.

Where sufficient normal training observations are available,
allow the user to select a threshold using baseline calibration.

Explain that an anomaly detector trained only on normal data
cannot reliably identify every possible fault type.

----------------------------------------------------
MODEL TRAINING AND VALIDATION
----------------------------------------------------

Separate training data from evaluation data.

Avoid training and testing on the same recordings.

Where possible, split recordings by recording session or source
so that nearly identical segments do not leak between training
and evaluation sets.

Report:

- Number of baseline recordings.
- Number of evaluation recordings.
- Feature extraction status.
- Model type.
- Threshold configuration.
- Evaluation limitations.

If labelled normal and anomalous examples are available,
calculate appropriate metrics such as:

- Precision.
- Recall.
- F1 score.
- False-positive rate.
- Confusion matrix.

Only display these metrics when actual evaluation data supports
their calculation.

If labels are unavailable, explain that conventional supervised
classification metrics cannot be calculated reliably.

