10. FEW-SHOT AND LIMITED-DATA STRATEGY
====================================================

An important research direction for MachineSentry is anomaly
detection when only a small amount of operating data is available.

Do not claim to have solved few-shot learning merely because
the application uses an anomaly-detection model.

Build a small-data experiment mode.

Allow the user to select:

- Small baseline.
- Medium baseline.
- Larger baseline.

Where sufficient data exists, train and evaluate the same model
under these different training-data conditions.

Display:

- Number of baseline recordings.
- Anomaly-score distribution.
- Evaluation results, if valid.
- Changes in detection behaviour.
- Limitations caused by small datasets.

Use the same held-out evaluation set when comparing different
training sizes.

If there is not enough data for a valid experiment, display a
clear explanation rather than inventing results.

For the initial prototype, focus on reliable baseline-driven
anomaly detection before attempting advanced few-shot methods.

